          "content": "Teacher’s Note"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.178,
            0.569,
            0.87,
            0.682
          ],
          "content": "It is important that students see the application of each mathematical concept in their daily lives. Teacher can organise some activities where students can appreciate the practical applications of angles in real-life situations, e.g., clocks, doors, swings, concepts of uphill and downhill, location of the sun, the giving of directions, etc."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.166,
            0.718,
            0.466,
            0.744
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "2.10 Drawing Angles"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.166,
            0.758,
            0.882,
            0.781
          ],
          "content": "Vidya wants to draw a 30° angle and name it ∠TIN using a protractor."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.179,
            0.802,
            0.875,
            0.869
          ],
          "content": "In ∠TIN, I will be the vertex, IT and IN will be the arms of the angle. Keeping one arm, say IN, as the reference (base), the other arm IT should take a turn of 30°."
        },
